<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Cimmerians</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 630 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Horse-riding raiders of the Pontic steppe whose attacks shook Urartu, Phrygia and Lydia in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Cimmerians are known almost entirely from outside sources: Assyrian letters calling them Gimirri, a line in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and the later accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who placed their homeland north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Archaeologists still debate which steppe cultures belonged to them. They appear in history around 714 BCE, when they defeated the army of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Urartu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were mounted nomads led by war chiefs, apparently speaking an Iranian language. Pushed south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, they became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and raiders across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. They overran <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-phrygia">Phrygia</a> around 696 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">King Midas</a> is said to have killed himself, and later sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges of Lydia</a> around 644 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugdamme">Dugdamme</a>, the Lygdamis of Greek sources, died campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> around 640 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes of Lydia</a> finally drove the remaining bands out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Survivors merged with neighbouring peoples. Their name lived on in Greek legend, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Cimmerian Bosporus</a> and, through Welsh antiquarians, in the mistaken idea that they were ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welsh-people">Cymry</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cimmerians.jpg" alt="Cimmerians" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Antiquistik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cimmerian_Migrations.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 630 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Teushpa, Dugdamme (Lygdamis), Sandakshatra</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably an Iranian language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cimmerian confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-815&amp;focus=cimmerians" aria-label="Cimmerians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5419 439 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cimmerians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5768 1035h0M5813 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5739 695h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5739 695h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ukraine. <a href="/atlas?year=-815&amp;focus=cimmerians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
